<section style="padding-top:40px">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px">

    <div class="guide-toc">
      <h4>In this guide</h4>
      <a href="#not-enemy">Repetition is not the enemy</a>
      <a href="#clusters">Why club nights naturally form clusters</a>
      <a href="#cost">The hidden cost of accidental repetition</a>
      <a href="#maths">Why perfect variety is impossible</a>
      <a href="#newcomers">Mixing newcomers well</a>
      <a href="#strength">Variety versus game balance</a>
      <a href="#requests">Friends, league pairs and requested games</a>
      <a href="#manual">A practical manual rule</a>
      <a href="#software">How software should treat repeats</a>
      <a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:34px">

      <p class="lead"><strong>Variety is valuable because a club night is a network, not because every combination must be unique.</strong> The aim is to stop repeated pairings becoming the default path through the evening while still allowing friendships, useful partnerships and good matches to exist.</p>

      <h2 id="not-enemy">Repetition is not the enemy</h2>

      <p>Badminton partnerships improve through familiarity. You learn which cross-court shuttle your partner will leave, who takes the middle under pressure and whether they are about to rotate behind you. Repeated play can therefore be useful badminton.</p>

      <p>It is also social. If two people joined the club together, there is no virtue in deliberately keeping them apart all night merely to improve a diversity statistic.</p>

      <p>The problem is <strong>unexamined repetition</strong>: the same combinations keep reappearing because they are convenient, not because anyone chose them for a purpose.</p>

      <p>A good variety policy should be able to distinguish:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>intentional repetition:</strong> a league pair practising together;</li>
        <li><strong>harmless repetition:</strong> two friends happen to partner twice in six games;</li>
        <li><strong>structural repetition:</strong> a queue keeps sending the same block of four back together;</li>
        <li><strong>social exclusion:</strong> a subgroup repeatedly selects itself and leaves other suitable players outside it.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="clusters">Why club nights naturally form clusters</h2>

      <p>Left alone, people do not mix randomly.</p>

      <p>Friends stand together. Players of similar standard recognise one another. Stronger players often prefer rallies where the shuttle comes back. Beginners may feel safer with people they know. An organiser under time pressure reaches for four names that obviously work.</p>

      <p>The queue can amplify this. If four players finish together and all join the back together, they may reach the front together again. With several courts turning over in blocks, the session can develop little cohorts that orbit the queue at roughly the same speed.</p>

      <p>None of this requires bad behaviour. It is a stable outcome produced by convenience.</p>

      <h2 id="cost">The hidden cost of accidental repetition</h2>

      <p>The first cost is obvious: <strong>staleness</strong>. A club of twenty-five people can somehow feel like a club of six if your games keep recycling the same social neighbourhood.</p>

      <p>The second is informational. Playing different people tells you more about a player's level. If Alice always partners Ben, their results reveal a lot about Alice-and-Ben and less about Alice independently. This matters if the club uses results for ratings or team selection.</p>

      <p>The third is social access. A newcomer does not need to play everybody on night one, but they do need routes into the existing network. If every appropriate player is already part of an informal fixed four, the newcomer can be present in the hall without becoming part of the club.</p>

      <p>That does not prove repetition causes somebody to leave - retention is far more complicated than that. It does mean deliberate mixing is one of the few low-cost things an organiser can do to make social access less dependent on existing friendships.</p>

      <h2 id="maths">Why perfect variety is impossible</h2>

      <p>Each player has only a finite number of possible partners.</p>

      <p>In a group of 8, one player has 7 possible partners. If they play 6 games, avoiding every repeat is possible in theory - but not once you also require fair waits, sensible standards and everyone else's partner histories to work at the same time.</p>

      <p>In a group of 6, a player has only 5 possible partners. A sixth game <em>must</em> repeat somebody.</p>

      <p>And partner variety is only half the problem. Each doubles game also creates two opponents. Optimising both simultaneously becomes a combinatorial scheduling problem very quickly.</p>

      <p>This is why “never give me the same partner twice” is a poor hard rule. The system eventually has to choose between breaking the rule, making somebody wait unfairly or creating a poor game.</p>

      <p>A better objective is:</p>

      <p><strong>avoid recent and excessive repeats when a comparably good alternative exists.</strong></p>

      <h2 id="newcomers">Mixing newcomers well</h2>

      <p>"Mix the newcomer in" can go wrong in two opposite directions.</p>

      <p>At one extreme, all new or developing players are put together. They meet nobody established and may get a low-quality game because nobody can stabilise the rally.</p>

      <p>At the other, the newcomer is deliberately cycled through every level in the club in the name of inclusion, including games that are far beyond or below them.</p>

      <p>Start by estimating standard. Then give them several different <em>appropriate</em> partners and opponents. A stronger, welcoming regular can be an excellent partner because they can absorb variation without making the game one-sided - but do not turn that one helpful member into the permanent newcomer chaperone.</p>

      <p>Social mixing works best when it looks like ordinary badminton rather than an intervention.</p>

      <h2 id="strength">Variety versus game balance</h2>

      <p>Suppose the best-balanced next game repeats one partnership from twenty minutes ago. The most varied alternative creates a large mismatch.</p>

      <p>For an ordinary club night, take the better game.</p>

      <p>Variety should usually sit below:</p>

      <ol>
        <li>gross court-time fairness;</li>
        <li>basic game viability;</li>
        <li>then recent partner/opponent repetition.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>That does not make variety unimportant. It makes it a <strong>tie-breaker among acceptable games</strong> rather than a reason to manufacture bad ones.</p>

      <p>This hierarchy becomes more important in mixed-ability clubs. There may only be a handful of combinations that create a close contest. Repetition is sometimes the price of not producing another 21-6.</p>

      <h2 id="requests">Friends, league pairs and requested games</h2>

      <p>Player preference is part of a social club, not an inconvenience to optimise away.</p>

      <p>Let friends request an occasional game. Let a league pair stay together for practice. Let someone have the rematch they have been talking about since last Thursday.</p>

      <p>The boundary is frequency and effect. If a fixed pair consumes six games in a session while several players never get an appropriate game with either of them, a personal preference has become a club policy without anybody voting for it.</p>

      <p>A useful convention is to mark intentional fixed partnerships explicitly. Then the organiser or software can protect those games while still treating the pair normally in the rest of the rotation.</p>

      <h2 id="manual">A practical manual rule</h2>

      <p>You do not need a spreadsheet of every partnership.</p>

      <p>When choosing the next four:</p>

      <ol>
        <li>start with the players who are fairly due a game;</li>
        <li>make the best balanced combination among them;</li>
        <li>ask whether either partnership has happened very recently;</li>
        <li>if a simple swap preserves the game quality, make it;</li>
        <li>if not, accept the repeat and move on.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>Keep a closer eye on patterns than individual duplicates. "They partnered twice" is weak evidence. "Those four have formed four of their last five games together while twelve similar-standard players are available" is a pattern worth breaking.</p>

      <h2 id="software">How software should treat repeats</h2>

      <p>Software has one enormous advantage: memory. It can see recent partners and opponents without relying on the organiser to remember Game 3 while choosing Game 17.</p>

      <p>But memory only helps if the objective is sensible. A picker should normally treat repetition as a penalty that fades with time, not a permanent ban.</p>

      <p><a href="/features/club-nights">ePegboard</a> includes recent repeats and broader partner/opponent variety among the signals considered alongside wait fairness and expected game quality. That means a fresh combination is preferred when it does not cost too much elsewhere, rather than variety winning at any price.</p>

      <p>This is also why storing history can matter beyond one evening. A pair who did not meet tonight may still have played together for most of last month. Whether cross-session history should count strongly is a club preference - and in a large club it can be more useful than remembering only the last two hours.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout">
        <h4>The test for useful variety</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">At the end of the night, players should feel they met enough different badminton to keep the session interesting - without being able to point to a string of poor games created solely to make every combination unique.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      @foreach (var f in faqPairs)
      {
